[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports show <ProblemCount ticket={4471} /> problems. The queued parser fix closes only one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below needs its own fix and its own ticket.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

## How the reports split

1. **Parser** (<ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" /> reports). The shared parser reads every
   date month first. Our tenants write the day first, so day and month swap: "booked for 7 April,
   the invite says 4 July". This is the problem #4471 describes.
2. **Form hint** (<ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" /> reports). The booking-v3 form prints
   `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first tenants type what the hint says, and the
   form reads the date the tenant's way. The result looks the same as the parser bug, but only
   reports sent through the form at booking-v3 tenants have it. The parser fix leaves it open.
3. **One-day move** (<ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="shift" /> reports). The date moves by one
   day, not by a day/month swap: "invite says Tuesday 12 May, calendar says Monday 11 May", "client
   in Lisbon got Tuesday, client in Toronto got Monday". The desk read these as a one-day move
   (desk notes) but counted them under the parser. A swap fix cannot close them; this points to
   time zones.

## Why they were merged

The desk merges on subject alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). These
problems share subjects such as "Date is wrong", so the rule put them in one ticket. Intake tags do
not help either: intake guesses from the browser, and the cause depends on the tenant and the form.

## What changes

- #4471 keeps the parser problem only.
- The form hint and the one-day move each get their own ticket, linked in the table above.
- Matching on subject merges different problems. A new decision record should replace 0001.

